Lab: Configure an Application for Reliability
Objective
•	Ensure application reliability by defining:
o	Memory resource requests and limits
o	Readiness probes to prevent routing traffic to unready pods
o	Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) to scale based on memory usage
•	Test scaling behavior under simulated memory load.
________________________________________
2. Lab Setup
lab start reliability-review
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
oc project reliability-review
•	The longload application is pre-deployed but fails due to high memory requests (8 GiB).
________________________________________
3. Diagnose Memory Issue
•	List pods:
oc get pods
•	Pod is Pending due to insufficient memory.
•	Describe pod to check events:
oc describe pod <pod-name>
•	Confirm deployment memory request:
oc get deployment longload -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources.requests.memory}{"\n"}'
•	Output: 8Gi (too high for available nodes).
________________________________________
4. Set Correct Memory Requests
•	Reduce memory request to 512 MiB:
oc set resources deployment/longload --requests memory=512Mi
watch oc get pods
•	Wait for pod to enter Running status.
________________________________________
5. Verify Application Readiness
•	Run looped curl requests:
~/DO180/labs/reliability-review/curl_loop.sh
•	Observe application eventually reports Ok.
________________________________________
6. Scale Application and Observe Failures
•	Scale to 3 replicas:
oc scale deployment/longload --replicas 3
•	Some requests fail because new pods are not ready.
•	Application takes ~7 seconds to initialize.
________________________________________
7. Configure Readiness Probe
•	Prevent routing traffic to unready pods:
oc set probe deployment/longload --readiness \
  --initial-delay-seconds 7 \
  --get-url http://:3000/health
•	Scale back to 1 pod, then test scaling up again:
oc scale deployment/longload --replicas 3
•	Curl loop now shows no failed requests.
________________________________________
8. Configure Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
•	Scale based on memory utilization:
o	Min replicas: 1
o	Max replicas: 3
o	Target average memory utilization: 60%
•	Example HPA YAML (hpa.yml):
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: longload
  labels:
    app: longload
spec:
  maxReplicas: 3
  minReplicas: 1
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: longload
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 60
•	Apply HPA:
oc apply -f ~/DO180/labs/reliability-review/hpa.yml
watch oc get hpa longload
________________________________________
9. Test HPA Behavior
1.	Simulate memory load (480 MiB per request):
oc exec deploy/longload -- curl -s localhost:3000/leak
•	Observe HPA scales up pods as memory usage exceeds 60%.
2.	Free memory:
~/DO180/labs/reliability-review/free.sh
•	Observe HPA scales down pods once memory usage drops.
________________________________________
10. Lab Cleanup
•	Grade work:
lab grade reliability-review
•	Finish exercise:
lab finish reliability-review
________________________________________
Key Takeaways
•	Memory requests must match application requirements for scheduling.
•	Readiness probes prevent routing to pods that are still starting.
•	Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) can scale pods automatically based on CPU or memory metrics.
•	HPA scaling up is faster than scaling down.
•	Load testing is essential to validate HPA behavior.


